<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<cfsSimulation xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
               xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation ../../../../../share/xml/CFS-Simulation/CFS.xsd"
               xmlns="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation">

  <documentation>
    <title>Coulomb friction: a sticking interface carries the applied tangential load</title>
    <authors><author>kroppert</author></authors>
    <date>2026-08-06</date>
    <keywords><keyword>contact</keyword></keywords>
    <references>none</references>
    <isVerified>no</isVerified>
    <description>
      Gate for the STICK branch of the Coulomb return map.

      The ContactPatchTest2d geometry: two QUAD9 blocks with a coincident interface at y = 1,
      the lower body fixed at the bottom. The upper body has NO displacement boundary condition
      at all, so BOTH its supports run through the contact interface: the normal one through the
      penalty, the tangential one through friction. That is what makes the tangential number a
      statement about friction rather than about a reaction the solver would have produced
      anyway.

      Loads on its top face: p_N = 1e6 Pa and p_T = 5e4 Pa in x, so p_T/p_N = 0.05 against mu =
      0.4 -- an eightfold margin inside the cone, so every point must stick. Equilibrium fixes
      both resultants exactly, independent of the elasticity solution and of the penalties,
      because the interface is the only load path in either direction:

      totalContactForce = p_N * L = 2e6 N/m totalTangentialForce = p_T * L = 1e5 N/m

      and numSlipPoints must be 0.

      The margin is not decoration: the tangential traction acts a block height above the
      interface, so it carries a moment and the pressure comes out linear in x. Raising p_T to
      2e5 tilts it hard enough that the windward point leaves the cone -- that is
      ContactFrictionPartialSlip2d, a separate test on purpose. Note that maxTangentialTraction
      exceeds p_T because the block shears; only the RESULTANT is fixed by equilibrium, which is
      why that is what is asserted.
    </description>
  </documentation>

  <fileFormats>
    <input>
      <hdf5 fileName="ContactFrictionStick2d.h5" linearizeEntities="__none__"/>
    </input>
    <output><hdf5/></output>
    <materialData file="mat.xml" format="xml"/>
  </fileFormats>

  <domain geometryType="plane">
    <regionList>
      <region name="master_elem" material="Aluminium"/>
      <region name="slave_elem" material="Aluminium"/>
    </regionList>
    <surfRegionList>
      <surfRegion name="master_iface"/>
      <surfRegion name="slave_iface"/>
      <surfRegion name="top"/>
      <surfRegion name="bottom"/>
    </surfRegionList>
  </domain>

  <fePolynomialList>
    <Lagrange><gridOrder/></Lagrange>
  </fePolynomialList>

  <integrationSchemeList>
    <scheme>
      <method>Gauss</method>
      <order>4</order>
      <mode>absolute</mode>
    </scheme>
  </integrationSchemeList>

  <sequenceStep>
    <analysis><static/></analysis>

    <pdeList>
      <mechanic subType="planeStrain">
        <regionList>
          <region name="master_elem"/>
          <region name="slave_elem"/>
        </regionList>

        <contactList>
          <contactPair name="block_contact"
                       primarySurface="master_iface"
                       secondarySurface="slave_iface"
                       slidingType="small"
                       normalPenalty="100"
                       initialGapHandling="fromMesh"
                       integrationOrder="2">
            <!-- mu = 0.4 against an applied ratio of 0.05: an eightfold margin, so the
                 stick verdict is not a near-miss of the cone. -->
            <friction type="coulomb" mu="0.4" tangentialPenalty="100"/>
          </contactPair>
        </contactList>

        <bcsAndLoads>
          <!-- lower body fully supported -->
          <fix name="bottom">
            <comp dof="x"/><comp dof="y"/>
          </fix>
          <!-- The upper body is deliberately left completely free. Its x support is the
               friction stick stiffness and its y support the contact penalty; remove the
               friction and the tangential system is singular. -->
          <pressure name="top" value="1e6"/>
          <traction name="top"><comp dof="x" value="5e4"/></traction>
        </bcsAndLoads>

        <storeResults>
          <nodeResult type="mechDisplacement"><allRegions/></nodeResult>
        </storeResults>
      </mechanic>
    </pdeList>

    <linearSystems>
      <system>
        <solutionStrategy>
          <standard>
            <setup idbcHandling="penalty"/>
            <matrix storage="sparseNonSym" reordering="noReordering"/>
            <nonLinear method="newton" logging="no">
              <lineSearch type="none"/>
              <incStopCrit>1e-9</incStopCrit>
              <resStopCrit>1e-9</resStopCrit>
              <maxNumIters>20</maxNumIters>
            </nonLinear>
          </standard>
        </solutionStrategy>
        <solverList><directLU/></solverList>
      </system>
    </linearSystems>

  </sequenceStep>
</cfsSimulation>
